e  dinner  oh I've eaten what did you eat gluten  free potato chips my mind went if you  understand where gluten comes from and  what food contains gluten you would  already know that potato has no gluten  and any potato chips you buy will be  gluten free like I said it's just  another marketing gimmick to put the  gluten free label onto a product to make  us think that it is healthy gluten-free  cookies muffins or cakes look processed  foods where the gluten free or not are  not good for us and can cause weight  gain it doesn't give you the allowance  to consume more and it is not a good  option for weight loss if you read the  ingredients list it will still contain  high sugar unhealthy fat and calories  sometimes a gluten free products may  even contain more sugar fats and  calories  just to make up for the lack of taste if  you want to reduce your gluten free  intake opt for naturally gluten-free  foods like meats nuts fruits and  vegetables instead of processed  gluten-free foods the next healthy food  granola  if you're eating granola for breakfast  you've got to stop it is constantly  being advertised as a healthier choice  but the reality is it is not it should  be treated as dessert because the sugar  content is as high as desert hence  should only be eaten occasionally yes  granola contains a lot of fiber and iron  from oats while the nuts and seeds add  heart-healthy unsaturated fats however  they are very high in calories because  they contain additional oil and sugar  which we just don't need  a quarter cup of granola typically  contains more than 200 calories and  about 20 grams of sugar that's just like  having chocolate éclairs and trust me  that one small portion will not fill you  up so chances are you will be consuming  double or triple that portion so  breakfast can rack up to 700 calories  and 60 grams of sugar and you're  wondering why am I not losing weight I'm  consuming granola for breakfast here's a  tip opt for muesli instead or even  better make your own where you're able  to add more rolled oats and have the  nuts seeds and dried fruits in small  quantities plus it is so much cheaper  vitamin sports or energy drinks these  are often sneaky and wasted calories  that we just don't need most of the time  sports drinks were designed with high  performance athletes in mind these  drinks contain electrolytes aka salt and  carbs aka sugar to keep them hydrated  and replenish lost minerals such as  sodium potassium and chloride especially  after a prolonged intense exercise now  hitting the treadmill for half an hour  and then lifting some weights does not  require nearly the same amount of carbs  or electrolytes we can get these  nutrients from the food we normally eat  this vitamin spot or energy drinks  contain just as much and sometimes even  more sugar than a can of soda if you  want vitamin carry through supplements  not some sort of a vitamin drink that  contains a whopping 33 grams of sugar if  you really feel that you need that extra  energy boost for your workout then fill  your body with enough healthy carbs and  protein before and after such as bananas  yogurt oatmeal eggs or go for  supplements such as BCAA or a protein  shake these are much healthier options  compared to a sugary drink and just stay  high  on plain refreshing water and finally  100% real vegetable chips made from 100%  real vegetable well potato chips is also  made from 100% real potato if you think  that you can feel less guilty enjoying  eating kale bits of zucchini don't also  forget about the vegetable oil salt and  potato starch added into the mixture to  produce real 100% veggie chips here's a  comparison one ounce serving of  vegetable chips contains 134 calories  and 6 point 6 grams of fat of which 0.5  grams is saturated and 1 ounce serving  of potato chips contains 154 calories  and ten point three grams of fat of  which 1.1 grams are saturated yes it is  a slightly better option but it's not  going to do your diet 